   title: Lock lead time
   audience: all
   summary: Set the lead time for MIDI parameter locks before their step.
-  prose: 'Some synths smooth incoming CC values. A filter or level change sent at the same moment as a note then arrives late and glides through the start of the note. Lock lead time sends each step''s lock values slightly before the step so they have settled when the note sounds. The notes themselves keep their timing.
-
-
-    1. Tap K1 briefly and open the norns PARAMS menu.
-
-    2. Open MOSAIC and select **Lock lead time (ms)** in the Parameter locks section.
-
-    3. Turn E3 to set the required lead time. The menu displays the selected value.
-
-    4. Return to Mosaic to compare playback.
-
-
-    Worked values at 130 BPM, where one clock pulse lasts about 4.8 ms. A lead of 0 sends each lock value with its note. A lead of 25 sends it about 28.8 ms ahead (6 pulses). A lead of 50 sends it about 52.9 ms ahead (11 pulses). Listen for the filter or parameter change landing before the note starts instead of sweeping through its attack.
-
-    This global setting accepts 0–50 ms and defaults to **25 ms**. It gives MIDI CC/NRPN
-
-    parameter locks time to settle before the note attack, particularly on devices
-
-    that smooth incoming controllers. Set it to **0** to send each value at its own
-
-    step, which is the original output timing. The value is saved with Mosaic''s
-
-    project settings.
-
-
-    A step''s parameter values leave ahead of the step, in an earlier clock pulse.
-
-    Nothing is delayed to achieve this: note-ons, note-offs, live MIDI-thru, outgoing
-
-    MIDI Clock, Start, Continue, Stop and song position all keep the timing they have
-
-    at a lead of 0, and gate lengths are unchanged. N.b. players are unaffected.
-
-
-    Because a value leaves on a clock pulse, the wait it achieves is the setting
-
-    rounded **up** to the next whole pulse, so a value never leaves with less lead
-
-    than was asked for. At 130 bpm a pulse is about 4.8 ms, so a 25 ms setting
-
-    delivers about 28.8 ms; at 200 bpm it delivers 25 ms exactly. The figure follows
-
-    the tempo, and every value of a step leaves on the same pulse, so what you hear
-
-    stays consistent between events rather than fixed to the number in the menu.
-
-
-    No step is looked ahead musically: probability, conditions, fills, random notes,
-
-    strum, arps and micro-timing still make their decisions at their own times. Only
-
-    the parameter values a step will send are resolved early.
-
-
-    Every note sounds with its own step''s values. When two notes on the same MIDI
-
-    channel are less than twice the lead apart (a fast tempo, a fast channel clock,
-
-    or swing and shuffle bringing steps together), a value does not take the whole
-
-    lead: it waits until halfway between the previous sounding note and its own step.
-
-    The previous note keeps its value through the first half of the gap, and the new
-
-    value settles in the second half. A trigless step sounds nothing, so it is not
-
-    the note a later value waits behind. A strummed chord''s later voices are sounding
-
-    notes, so the next step''s values wait behind the last of them.
-
-
-    An address that more than one slot writes, whether two slots of one channel or
-
-    two channels addressing one device parameter, is not sent early: its values leave
-
-    at their own step, in the order the step would send them at a lead of 0.
-
-
-    Two cases give no lead at all. The first step after Play resolves its lock on the
-
-    transport''s own first pulse, so there is no earlier pulse for its values to leave
-
-    in and they leave with the note. A parameter that is mid-slide keeps its slide
-
-    until the slide''s destination step, so that step''s lock arrives with the step
-
-    rather than before it. Nothing is delayed to hide either case.
-
-
-    The lead covers the time a receiver takes for a parameter change to take effect,
-
-    so every value follows the same rule whether or not its step has a note: trigless
-
-    locks, assigned values and slide values leave ahead of the step they belong to and
-
-    take effect as that step is heard.
-
-
-    Editing a lock that has already left changes what the next pass sends, and the
-
-    edited value is sent again at its own step, so the receiver is corrected rather
-
-    than left holding the superseded value. Mosaic restores its output hooks on
-
-    cleanup; stop and panic release held voices without leaving queued output behind.'
+  prose: "Some synths smooth incoming CC values. A filter or level change sent at the same moment as a note then arrives late and glides through the start of the note. Lock lead time sends each step's lock values slightly before the step so they have settled when the note sounds. The notes themselves keep their timing.\n\n1. Tap K1 briefly and open the norns PARAMS menu.\n2. Open MOSAIC and select **Lock lead time (ms)** in the Parameter locks section.\n3. Turn E3 to set the required lead time. The menu displays the selected value.\n4. Return to Mosaic to compare playback.\n\nWorked values at 130 BPM, where one clock pulse lasts about 4.8 ms. A lead of 0 sends each lock value with its note. A lead of 25 sends it about 28.8 ms ahead (6 pulses). A lead of 50 sends it about 52.9 ms ahead (11 pulses). Listen for the filter or parameter change landing before the note starts instead of sweeping through its attack.\nThis global setting accepts 0–50 ms and defaults to **25 ms**. It gives MIDI CC/NRPN\nparameter locks time to settle before the note attack, particularly on devices\nthat smooth incoming controllers. Set it to **0** to send each value at its own\nstep, which is the original output timing. The value is saved with Mosaic's\nproject settings.\n\nA step's parameter values leave ahead of the step, in an earlier clock pulse.\nNothing is delayed to achieve this: note-ons, note-offs, live MIDI-thru, outgoing\nMIDI Clock, Start, Continue, Stop and song position all keep the timing they have\nat a lead of 0, and gate lengths are unchanged. N.b. players are unaffected.\n\nBecause a value leaves on a clock pulse, the wait it achieves is the setting\nrounded **up** to the next whole pulse, so a value never leaves with less lead\nthan was asked for. At 130 bpm a pulse is about 4.8 ms, so a 25 ms setting\ndelivers about 28.8 ms; at 200 bpm it delivers 25 ms exactly. The figure follows\nthe tempo, and every value of a step leaves on the same pulse, so what you hear\nstays consistent between events rather than fixed to the number in the menu.\n\nNo step is looked ahead musically: probability, conditions, fills, random notes,\nstrum, arps and micro-timing still make their decisions at their own times. Only\nthe parameter values a step will send are resolved early.\n\nEvery note sounds with its own step's values. When two notes on the same MIDI\nchannel are less than twice the lead apart (a fast tempo, a fast channel clock,\nor swing and shuffle bringing steps together), a value does not take the whole\nlead: it waits until halfway between the previous sounding note and its own step.\nThe previous note keeps its value through the first half of the gap, and the new\nvalue settles in the second half. A trigless step sounds nothing, so it is not\nthe note a later value waits behind. A strummed chord's later voices are sounding\nnotes, so the next step's values wait behind the last of them.\n\nAn address that more than one slot writes, whether two slots of one channel or\ntwo channels addressing one device parameter, is not sent early: its values leave\nat their own step, in the order the step would send them at a lead of 0.\n\nTwo cases give no lead at all. The first step after Play resolves its lock on the\ntransport's own first pulse, so there is no earlier pulse for its values to leave\nin and they leave with the note. A parameter that is mid-slide keeps its slide\nuntil the slide's destination step, so that step's lock arrives with the step\nrather than before it. Nothing is delayed to hide either case.\n\nThe lead covers the time a receiver takes for a parameter change to take effect,\nso every value follows the same rule whether or not its step has a note: trigless\nlocks, assigned values and slide values leave ahead of the step they belong to and\ntake effect as that step is heard.\n\nEditing a lock that has already left changes what the next pass sends, and the\nedited value is sent again at its own step, so the receiver is corrected rather\nthan left holding the superseded value. Stopping playback or using Panic releases held voices and clears pending parameter-lock output."
   musical_use: Use 25 to 50 ms on a hardware synth whose filter or effect needs a moment to settle after a CC change. Use 0 when you want each value sent exactly at its own step.
